\viewkind4\uc1 \f1\fs20\b STUDY SHEET\b0\par
\f1\fs32\b The Firstfruit and the Honest Weight\b0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 Torah: Deuteronomy 26:1\u8211?29:9 | Haftarah: Isaiah 60:1\u8211?22 | Gospel: Matthew 4:13\u8211?24\par
\f0\fs22\i In Ki Tavo you bring the first of the harvest back because all is gift \u8212? down to the heart to understand (29:4); and the joy in it (28:47) is no atmosphere but the test of whether you still remember Who gave it.\i0\par
\par\f0\fs24 
\par\b\fs28 \u167?0. Observation round (order-safeguard)\b0\fs24\par


\par \i This round fixes the production order: first observe, then the axes as an open question, then read off the center of gravity, and only then the thread. The directional test is the horizon, not the starting point.\i0 \par


\par\b\fs24 0.1 Blind observation\b0\fs24\par


\par The parashah opens with the firstfruits (26:1\u8211?11): when you come into the land, take the first of all the fruit, put it in a basket, go to the place He chooses, and speak a confession \u8212? \u8216?a Syrian ready to perish was my father\u8217? (arami oved avi): we went down to Egypt, became a people, were oppressed, cried out, and the LORD brought us out with a strong hand into this land of milk and honey; and now I bring the first. And you shall rejoice in every good thing. Then the tithe confession (26:12\u8211?15), and a mutual declaration (26:16\u8211?19): you have said the LORD is your God, and the LORD has said you are His own people. Then the stones at Shechem, with all this Torah, and Gerizim/Ebal with twelve curses over mostly hidden sins, each answered with \u8216?Amen\u8217? (27). Then the great blessing and curse (28). And then (29:1\u8211?9): you have seen it all \u8212? the signs and wonders \u8212? \u8216?yet the LORD hath not given you an heart to perceive, and eyes to see, and ears to hear, unto this day\u8217? (29:4).\par


\par Recurring tension: everywhere the human is called to say and to do (the firstfruits confession, \u8216?I have hearkened\u8217?, \u8216?you have said\u8217?, the \u8216?Amen\u8217?, obey-and-be-blessed) \u8212? and then 29:4 says that the heart to truly understand this is no achievement of one\u8217?s own, but is given.\par


\par\b\fs24 0.2 The four axes as an open question\b0\fs24\par









\b Axis\b0 
\b What the text brings up\b0 
\b Weight\b0 




\b Will\b0 
Dominant in volume: the whole parashah is covenant response \u8212? bring the first (26:2), confess (26:5\u8211?10), remember, obey, rejoice (26:11), answer \u8216?Amen\u8217? (27:15\u8211?26).
Bearing.


\b Source\b0 
Equally bearing: everything is received \u8212? the land, the fruit, being a people \u8212? down to the heart to understand (29:4). The status \u8216?his own people\u8217? (am segulah) is pronounced by God (26:18).
Bearing \u8212? meets Will in thankfulness.


\b Coherence\b0 
Present but not the core: the twelve curses touch hidden sins (27:15\u8211?26) \u8212? wholeness, no gap between inside and outside; and blessing/curse is a coherence of consequences (28).
Supporting; honest as second pole.


\b Trajectory\b0 
\u8216?When you come into the land\u8217? (26:1); the forty years (29:5); \u8216?unto this day\u8217? (29:4).
Thin.


\b Reactivity\b0 
Weak; not foregrounded.
Marginal.




\par\b\fs24 0.3 Read-off center of gravity\b0\fs24\par


\par Read off, not chosen: it is about the covenant response as thankful remembrance \u8212? serving out of received abundance. The two bearing axes are Source (all is gift, down to the heart) and Will (confessing, remembering, obeying, rejoicing), and they meet in thankfulness. The firstfruits are the paradigm; 28:47 is the negative reverse (joyless service = the root of the curse); and 29:4 is the floor: even the heart to understand this is given.\par


\par\b\fs24 0.4 Framing test\b0\fs24\par


\par Did this come from the observation? Yes \u8212? the frame of the parashah (firstfruits at the front, 29:4 at the end) plus the diagnosis in 28:47 steer together toward the thankful, remembering heart.\par


\par Two things honestly. One: the axes (Source\u8211?Will) are the same as at Re\u8217?eh, and I must check that I am not imposing that template. Tested: the content is different and read from this text \u8212? Re\u8217?eh landed on \u8216?not in your own eyes but before His face\u8217?; Ki Tavo lands on thankful remembrance (arami oved avi, 28:47, 29:4). That two Deuteronomy parashot both turn on will-as-response is the nature of Deuteronomy, not import; the hinges here are Ki Tavo\u8217?s own.\par


\par Two: there is a real alternative reading I do not paper over \u8212? the twelve curses over hidden sins (27:15\u8211?26) and the blessing/curse consequences pull toward Coherence (wholeness, no gap between hidden and open). That pole is real in 27\u8211?28. But the frame and the diagnosis (28:47) press harder on the remembering heart than on integrity, so that is where it lands \u8212? on Source\u8211?Will, with the Coherence pull honestly named.\par


\par\b\fs28 \u167?1. The thread\b0\fs24\par


\par \b Bringing the first back \u8212? serving out of what you received.\b0  Everything in Ki Tavo is gift: the land, the fruit, being a people, down to the heart to understand (29:4). And precisely therefore the response is not an achievement but thankfulness: you bring the first back, with the story of grace on your lips, and you rejoice. Joyless, forgetful service (28:47) is the root of the curse; thankful remembrance the root of the blessing.\par


\par\b\fs28 \u167?2. Peshat per block\b0\fs24\par


\par\b\fs24 2.1 The firstfruits (26:1\u8211?11)\b0\fs24\par


\par When you come into the land, take the first of all the fruit in a basket, go to the chosen place, and give it to the priest. 26:5\u8211?10 \u8212? the confession \u8216?a Syrian ready to perish was my father\u8217?: Egypt, growing into a people, oppression, crying out, the exodus with a strong hand, the land \u8212? and now the first. \u8216?Rejoice in every good thing\u8217? (26:11).\par


\par\b\fs24 2.2 The tithe confession (26:12\u8211?15)\b0\fs24\par


\par The third-year tithe for Levite, stranger, fatherless and widow; \u8216?I have hearkened to the voice of the LORD\u8217? (26:14). Obedience as an audible answer.\par


\par\b\fs24 2.3 The mutual declaration (26:16\u8211?19)\b0\fs24\par


\par You have declared this day that the LORD is your God; and the LORD has declared that you are His own people (am segulah), set high above the nations.\par


\par\b\fs24 2.4 The stones and the curses (27)\b0\fs24\par


\par On entry, great stones plastered with lime, inscribed with all this Torah; an altar. Gerizim/Ebal; twelve curses \u8212? mostly over hidden sins \u8212? each answered with \u8216?Amen\u8217?.\par


\par\b\fs24 2.5 Blessing and curse (28)\b0\fs24\par


\par The blessings on obedience; the long, swelling curse on disobedience; and the key: \u8216?because thou servedst not the LORD thy God with joyfulness and with gladness of heart, for the abundance of all things\u8217? (28:47).\par


\par\b\fs24 2.6 \u8216?No heart to perceive\u8217? (29:1\u8211?9)\b0\fs24\par


\par You have seen it all \u8212? the signs, the wonders, the forty years \u8212? \u8216?yet the LORD hath not given you an heart to perceive, and eyes to see, and ears to hear, unto this day\u8217? (29:4). Keep the words of this covenant and do them.\par


\par\b\fs24 2.7 Haftarah \u8212? Isaiah 60:1\u8211?22\b0\fs24\par


\par \u8216?Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of the LORD is risen upon thee\u8217? (60:1). The light rises upon you and the nations come to that light \u8212? received light that is reflected back.\par


\par\b\fs24 2.8 Gospel \u8212? Matthew 4:13\u8211?24\b0\fs24\par


\par Yeshua in Capernaum, in \u8216?Galilee of the nations\u8217?: \u8216?the people which sat in darkness saw great light\u8217? (4:16, citing Isa. 9). The calling of the first disciples (leaving the nets, following), preaching and healing.\par


\par\b\fs28 \u167?3. The two bearing axes and their relation\b0\fs24\par


\par\b\fs24 3.1 Source \u8212? all is gift\b0\fs24\par


\par The land, the fruit, being a people: nothing is earned. Even the heart to understand is given or withheld (29:4), and the status \u8216?his own people\u8217? is pronounced by God (26:18). The human receives before he brings anything back.\par


\par\b\fs24 3.2 Will \u8212? the thankful response\b0\fs24\par


\par And precisely therefore the response is no self-achievement but thankfulness: bring the first, confess the story of grace, remember, obey, rejoice, answer \u8216?Amen\u8217?. The will is truly addressed \u8212? but as an answer, not as a source.\par


\par\b\fs24 3.3 The relation \u8212? thankful remembrance as hinge\b0\fs24\par


\par 26:5\u8211?10 \u8212? the firstfruits confession (arami oved avi): you bring the first back with the story of gift on your lips. Receiving and giving back in one act. 28:47 \u8212? joyless, forgetful service \u8216?for the abundance\u8217? is the root of the curse: the gift without the remembering turns. 29:4 \u8212? even the heart to understand this is gift; the will is addressed but not autonomous.\par


\par \i Honest limit: this two-axis reading too can tempt to a too-neat synthesis. The safeguard is that the firstfruits (front), 28:47 (mid-curse) and 29:4 (end) carry the figure themselves \u8212? not brought by the reader.\i0 \par


\par\b\fs28 \u167?4. Deepening: joy as the test\b0\fs24\par


\par The parashah makes joy not an atmosphere but a test. Two people can keep exactly the same commandments; the difference lies in 28:47 \u8212? with or without gladness, out of remembrance or out of habit. The firstfruits show where the joy comes from: not from what I achieved, but from what I received, told again at every harvest. And 29:4 lays bare the floor: where joy and understanding are absent, that is no failure of technique but a lack of the given heart. The test is thus not \u8216?do I do enough?\u8217? but \u8216?do I remember, and do I rejoice in the Giver?\u8217?\par


\par\b\fs28 \u167?5. The directional test as horizon\b0\fs24\par


\par Beneath all this lies the one question \u8212? received or grasped \u8212? as the ground. The firstfruits are the received-form: the first back, because all is given. The joyless \u8216?my own strength has gotten me this\u8217? service (cf. 8:17; 28:47) is the grasped-form. And in the gospel the horizon becomes visible: the light rises (Isa. 60; Matt. 4:16), and the eyes 29:4 still withheld open in Yeshua \u8212? the given heart, embodied.\par


